// 2010 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2010\2010.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2010, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"A geographic area at an altitude of about 400 metres has a hot and humid climate, with average maximum temperatures around 28–31°C, average minimum temperatures around 20–21°C and substantial rainfall throughout the year. If this area were to have a natural forest, which one would it most likely be?","A":"Moist temperate coniferous forest","B":"Montane subtropical forest","C":"Temperate forest","D":"Tropical rain forest","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Forest Types","Climate","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The question describes a region with high rainfall and warm temperatures.\nAt the specified altitude, the source associates these climatic conditions with a moist temperate coniferous forest.\nForest type is determined by the combination of temperature, rainfall and altitude.\nTherefore, the correct answer according to the source is (a).","revisionPoints":"Forest distribution depends mainly on temperature, precipitation and altitude.\nConiferous forests are generally associated with cooler climatic conditions.\nTropical rainforests require high temperature and high rainfall.\nAlways correlate climate data with natural vegetation in geography questions."},
{"no":2,"text":"If a potato is placed on a pure white paper plate and put in a microwave oven, the potato heats up but the paper plate does not. Why?","A":"Potato is mainly made up of starch whereas paper is mainly made up of cellulose","B":"Potato transmits microwaves whereas paper reflects microwaves","C":"Potato contains water whereas paper does not contain water","D":"Potato is a fresh organic material whereas paper is a dead organic material","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Microwave","Physics","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Microwave heating occurs mainly through interaction of microwaves with polar molecules, especially water.\nPotato contains a substantial amount of water.\nThe dry paper plate absorbs much less microwave energy under these conditions.\nHence, the potato heats considerably more than the paper plate.","revisionPoints":"Microwave ovens heat food primarily through interaction with water molecules.\nWater is a polar molecule.\nMicrowave heating differs from conventional heating because energy is deposited within the food.\nMoist foods generally heat more readily than dry materials."},
{"no":3,"text":"With reference to the Constitution of India, which of the following provisions are fulfilled by the National Social Assistance Programme (NSAP)?\nFundamental Rights\nFundamental Duties\nDirective Principles of State Policy","A":"1 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["National Social Assistance Programme","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"NSAP provides social assistance to vulnerable sections such as elderly persons, widows and persons in need of support.\nSuch welfare measures reflect the objectives contained in the Directive Principles of State Policy.\nFundamental Duties are duties imposed on citizens and are not the constitutional basis identified for NSAP.\nThe source therefore identifies only statement 3.","revisionPoints":"Directive Principles → Part IV of the Constitution.\nDPSPs guide the State toward social and economic welfare.\nNSAP → social assistance programme for vulnerable groups.\nDPSPs are generally non-justiciable but fundamental in governance."},
{"no":4,"text":"Consider the following statements about El Niño Modoki:\nNormal El Niño forms in the Central Pacific whereas El Niño Modoki forms in the Eastern Pacific.\nNormal El Niño results in diminished hurricanes in the Atlantic, whereas El Niño Modoki results in a greater number of hurricanes with greater frequency.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["El Nino Modoki","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"El Niño Modoki is characterised by unusual warming concentrated more toward the central Pacific, rather than being identical to conventional El Niño.\nTherefore, statement 1 is incorrect.\nThe source identifies statement 2 as the correct distinction regarding Atlantic hurricane activity.\nEl Niño events can alter atmospheric circulation far beyond the Pacific.","revisionPoints":"El Niño → abnormal warming of tropical Pacific waters.\nEl Niño Modoki → different spatial pattern of Pacific warming.\nENSO influences global atmospheric circulation.\nEl Niño can affect rainfall, temperature and tropical cyclone activity in different regions."},
{"no":5,"text":"MON 863, a variety of maize, was in the news because:","A":"It is a genetically modified dwarf variety resistant to drought","B":"It is a genetically modified variety which is pest resistant","C":"It is a genetically modified variety with ten times higher protein content than regular maize","D":"It is a genetically modified variety used exclusively for bio-fuel production","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Mon 863","GM Maize","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"MON 863 is a genetically modified maize variety.\nIts genetic modification was associated with resistance to certain insect pests.\nPest-resistant genetically modified crops are designed to reduce crop damage caused by target insects.\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Genetically modified crops → organisms whose genetic material is deliberately altered.\nBt technology is widely associated with insect resistance.\nGM crops can be developed for traits such as pest resistance or herbicide tolerance.\nAgricultural biotechnology can affect productivity, pest management and farm inputs."},
{"no":6,"text":"A cuboid has six sides of different colours. The red side is opposite to black. The blue side is adjacent to white. The brown side is adjacent to blue. The red side is face down. Which colour would be opposite to brown?","A":"Red","B":"Black","C":"White","D":"Blue","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Cuboid Puzzle","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"In a cuboid, each face has exactly one opposite face.\nRed and black are already an opposite pair.\nFrom the given spatial relationships, blue and white form the relevant adjacent configuration.\nThe source's spatial arrangement leads to white being opposite brown.","revisionPoints":"Cuboid → 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices.\nOpposite faces never share an edge.\nSpatial reasoning questions require tracking adjacency and opposite relationships.\nDraw a cube/cuboid diagram when solving such questions."},
{"no":7,"text":"The satellite Oceansat-2, launched by India, helps in:\nEstimating water vapour content in the atmosphere\nPredicting the onset of monsoons\nMonitoring pollution of coastal waters\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Oceansat-2","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Oceansat-2 was designed primarily for ocean and atmospheric observations.\nSatellite observations can provide information useful for studying atmospheric moisture.\nOcean observations contribute to understanding weather and monsoon-related processes.\nCoastal and ocean-colour observations can also help monitor marine and coastal conditions.","revisionPoints":"Oceansat → Indian Earth-observation satellite series.\nOcean satellites help monitor ocean colour, winds and marine conditions.\nSatellite data support weather and climate research.\nRemote sensing is important for monitoring large and inaccessible areas."},
{"no":8,"text":"Genetically modified Golden Rice has been engineered to meet human nutritional requirements. Which statement best describes Golden Rice?","A":"Grains fortified with genes providing three times higher yield per acre","B":"Grains contain pro-vitamin A which, upon ingestion, is converted to vitamin A in the body","C":"Modified genes cause synthesis of all nine essential amino acids","D":"Modified genes cause fortification of grains with vitamin D","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Golden Rice","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Golden Rice was genetically modified to produce beta-carotene, a precursor of vitamin A.\nBeta-carotene is converted into vitamin A in the human body.\nThe objective was to address vitamin A deficiency.\nThe golden colour of the rice is associated with the accumulation of carotenoid pigments.","revisionPoints":"Golden Rice → example of genetic modification for nutritional enhancement.\nContains pro-vitamin A carotenoids.\nIntended to help address vitamin A deficiency.\nBiofortification aims to improve the nutritional quality of food crops."},
{"no":9,"text":"In a tournament, 14 teams play league matches. If each team plays every other team once, how many matches are played?","A":"105","B":"91","C":"85","D":"78","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Every team plays every other team exactly once.\nNumber of matches = number of ways of choosing 2 teams from 14.\n\\(\\binom{14}{2} = \\frac{14 \\times 13}{2}\\)\nTherefore, total matches = 91.","revisionPoints":"Round-robin league → every participant plays every other participant.\nFormula: n(n−1)/2.\nFor 14 teams → \\(14×13÷2 = 91\\).\nSuch questions test combinations."},
{"no":10,"text":"The Supreme Court of India tenders advice to the President on matters of law or fact:\nOn its own initiative in larger public interest\nIf the President seeks such advice\nOnly if the matter relates to Fundamental Rights of citizens\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1 and 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Supreme Court","Advisory Jurisdiction","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Under Article 143, the President may refer a question of law or fact of public importance to the Supreme Court.\nThe Supreme Court may then provide its advisory opinion.\nThe Court does not independently initiate such proceedings merely because it considers them to be in the public interest.\nThe provision is not restricted only to Fundamental Rights.","revisionPoints":"Article 143 → advisory jurisdiction of Supreme Court.\nPresident may seek the Court's opinion on questions of law or fact.\nAdvisory opinion is distinct from the Court's original and appellate jurisdiction.\nThe reference must involve a question considered appropriate under Article 143."},
{"no":11,"text":"Chlorination is used for water purification. The disinfecting action of chlorine is mainly due to:","A":"Formation of hydrochloric acid when chlorine is added to water","B":"Formation of hypochlorous acid when chlorine is added to water","C":"Formation of nascent oxygen when chlorine is added to water","D":"Formation of hydrogen when chlorine is added to water","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Chlorination","Water Purification","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Chlorine reacts with water to form hypochlorous acid (HOCl) and hydrochloric acid.\nHypochlorous acid is a strong disinfecting agent.\nIt penetrates microorganisms and oxidises essential cellular components.\nThis provides the principal disinfecting effect of chlorination.","revisionPoints":"Chlorination → widely used for drinking-water disinfection.\nImportant active disinfectant → hypochlorous acid.\nChlorine can react with organic matter in water and form disinfection by-products.\nAdequate chlorine residual helps prevent microbial regrowth."},
{"no":12,"text":"With reference to Lok Adalats, which of the following statements is correct?","A":"Lok Adalats have jurisdiction to settle matters at the pre-litigation stage as well as matters pending before courts.","B":"Lok Adalats can deal only with civil matters and not criminal matters.","C":"Every Lok Adalat consists only of serving or retired judicial officers.","D":"None of the statements given above is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Lok Adalats","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Lok Adalats provide a mechanism for alternative dispute resolution.\nThey can take up disputes before litigation reaches a court.\nThey can also deal with cases that are already pending before courts, subject to applicable conditions.\nTheir objective is speedy and inexpensive settlement through compromise.","revisionPoints":"Lok Adalat → forum for amicable settlement of disputes.\nCan deal with pre-litigation and pending cases.\nFocuses on compromise and settlement.\nAwards of Lok Adalats have legal enforceability as provided by law."},
{"no":13,"text":"Which of the following can operate in the 2.4–2.5 GHz radio-frequency range?\nBluetooth device\nCordless phone\nMicrowave oven\nWi-Fi device","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Bluetooth","Wi-Fi","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The 2.4 GHz ISM band is used by several consumer and communication technologies.\nBluetooth operates in this band.\nMany Wi-Fi devices also use 2.4 GHz.\nMicrowave ovens also operate around this frequency, and compatible cordless communication devices may use the band.","revisionPoints":"2.4 GHz → widely used ISM frequency band.\nBluetooth → commonly uses 2.4 GHz.\nWi-Fi → commonly uses 2.4 GHz, with newer standards also using other bands.\nMicrowave ovens operate around 2.45 GHz."},
{"no":14,"text":"Consider the following statements about coffee and tea:\nCoffee requires a hot and humid tropical climate, whereas tea can be cultivated in both tropical and subtropical areas.\nCoffee is propagated by seeds, whereas tea is propagated only by stem cuttings.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Coffee","Tea Cultivation","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Coffee generally grows well in warm, humid tropical highlands.\nTea can be cultivated across suitable tropical and subtropical highland environments.\nCoffee is commonly propagated through seeds.\nTea is commonly propagated vegetatively through cuttings, particularly for maintaining desirable characteristics.","revisionPoints":"Coffee → warm, humid climate and often hill slopes.\nTea → warm, humid climate with well-distributed rainfall.\nBoth crops are associated with plantation agriculture.\nMajor Indian tea regions include Assam, West Bengal and the Nilgiris."},
{"no":15,"text":"In the context of international trade, the phrase “Special Safeguard Mechanisms” is frequently associated with:","A":"United Nations Environment Programme","B":"World Trade Organisation","C":"ASEAN–India Free Trade Agreement","D":"G-20 Summits","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Special Safeguard Mechanisms","WTO","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Special Safeguard Mechanism (SSM) is associated with WTO agricultural trade negotiations.\nIt is designed to allow developing countries to take certain temporary protective measures when import surges or price declines threaten domestic farmers.\nThe mechanism is part of debates around agricultural trade liberalisation.","revisionPoints":"SSM → associated with WTO agricultural negotiations.\nIntended mainly as a safeguard for developing-country farmers.\nCan be triggered under specified conditions relating to imports/prices.\nReflects the tension between trade liberalisation and domestic agricultural protection."},
{"no":16,"text":"Which one of the following terms indicates a mechanism used by commercial banks for providing credit to the government?","A":"Cash Credit Ratio","B":"Debt Service Obligation","C":"Liquidity Adjustment Facility","D":"Statutory Liquidity Ratio","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Statutory Liquidity Ratio","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Under SLR requirements, banks must maintain a specified proportion of their liabilities in liquid assets.\nThese assets can include government securities.\nBy investing in government securities, commercial banks provide a source of financing to the government.\nTherefore, SLR is the relevant mechanism among the options.","revisionPoints":"SLR → proportion of specified liabilities maintained in liquid assets.\nGovernment securities are an important component of SLR assets.\nSLR is a tool associated with banking regulation and monetary management.\nLAF is primarily a framework for liquidity management between the RBI and banks."},
{"no":17,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Trade Marks and Geographical Indications (GI):\nA Trade Mark is an individual/company's right, whereas a GI is a community's right.\nA Trade Mark can be licensed, whereas a GI cannot be licensed.\nA Trade Mark is assigned to manufactured goods, while a GI is assigned only to agricultural goods/products and handicrafts.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Intellectual Property","tags":["Geographical Indication","Trade Mark","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"A Trade Mark identifies goods or services associated with a particular proprietor.\nA GI identifies goods whose qualities or reputation are linked to a specific geographical origin.\nTrade Marks can be licensed.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because GIs can also apply to certain manufactured goods and other products, not only agricultural products and handicrafts.","revisionPoints":"GI → linked to a specific geographical origin.\nTrade Mark → identifies goods/services of a particular proprietor.\nIndia enacted the Geographical Indications of Goods (Registration and Protection) Act, 1999.\nExamples include Darjeeling Tea and Kanchipuram Silk."},
{"no":18,"text":"The Special Economic Zones (SEZ) Act, 2005 had which of the following objectives?\nDevelopment of infrastructure facilities\nPromotion of investment from foreign sources\nPromotion of exports of services only","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["SEZ Act 2005","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"SEZs are intended to promote investment, exports and economic activity through a favourable regulatory and infrastructure environment.\nInfrastructure development is an important objective.\nAttracting foreign investment is also an objective.\nThe policy is not restricted to services exports only.","revisionPoints":"SEZ → designated area with special economic and regulatory provisions.\nObjectives include investment, exports and employment generation.\nSEZs can support both goods and services.\nInfrastructure development is important for SEZ competitiveness."},
{"no":19,"text":"Which one of the following is an appropriate description of deflation?","A":"A sudden fall in the value of a currency against other currencies","B":"A persistent recession in both the financial and real sectors of the economy","C":"A persistent fall in the general price level of goods and services","D":"A fall in the rate of inflation over a period of time","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Deflation","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Deflation refers to a sustained decline in the general price level.\nIt is different from disinflation.\nDisinflation means the inflation rate is falling, while prices may still be increasing.\nCurrency depreciation is an exchange-rate phenomenon and is not deflation.","revisionPoints":"Deflation → persistent fall in general price level.\nInflation → persistent rise in general price level.\nDisinflation → slowing rate of inflation.\nDeflation can be associated with weak demand and falling economic activity."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nBiodiversity hotspots are located only in tropical regions.\nIndia has four biodiversity hotspots: Eastern Himalayas, Western Himalayas, Western Ghats and Andaman & Nicobar Islands.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biodiversity Hotspots","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Biodiversity hotspots are not restricted exclusively to tropical regions; hotspot regions can occur in different climatic zones.\nThe source's second statement is also incorrect because the classification of India's hotspot regions does not treat the Western Himalayas and Andaman & Nicobar Islands as four separate hotspots in the manner stated.\nIndia's recognised hotspot associations include the Himalaya, Indo-Burma, Western Ghats–Sri Lanka and Sundaland regions, with parts of India falling within these.","revisionPoints":"Biodiversity hotspot concept combines high endemism and significant habitat loss/threat.\nIndia is associated with parts of four global biodiversity hotspots.\nWestern Ghats–Sri Lanka is one such hotspot.\nParts of the Himalaya, Indo-Burma and Sundaland hotspots also extend into India."},
{"no":21,"text":"Why is the malaria vaccine difficult to develop?","A":"Malaria is caused by several species of Plasmodium.","B":"The Plasmodium undergoes different stages in its life cycle.","C":"The parasite rapidly develops resistance to drugs.","D":"Man is only an intermediate host and not the definitive host.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Malaria","Vaccine Development","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"According to the source, the difficulty is associated with the complex host relationship of the malaria parasite.\nHumans act as the intermediate host.\nThe mosquito is the definitive host, where the sexual phase occurs.\nThe source identifies option (d) as the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Malaria is caused by Plasmodium.\nFemale Anopheles mosquito transmits malaria.\nHuman host: asexual multiplication.\nMosquito host: sexual reproduction."},
{"no":22,"text":"With reference to protected areas, consider:\nThe boundaries of a National Park are defined by legislation.\nA Biosphere Reserve is declared only to conserve a few specific species.\nIn a Wildlife Sanctuary, limited biotic interference is permitted.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["National Park","Biosphere Reserve","Wildlife Sanctuary","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"National Parks have boundaries established through legal notification.\nA Biosphere Reserve follows a broader ecosystem-based conservation approach; it is not established merely for a few species.\nCertain regulated human activities can be permitted in Wildlife Sanctuaries.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"National Park: stronger restrictions on human activities.\nWildlife Sanctuary: certain regulated activities may be permitted.\nBiosphere Reserve: conservation + sustainable use + research/education.\nBiosphere Reserves follow a broader landscape/ecosystem approach."},
{"no":23,"text":"A pesticide enters the food chain through food crops. Which sequence would show increasing concentration of the pesticide?","A":"Food crop → Rat → Snake → Hawk","B":"Food crop → Hawk → Snake → Rat","C":"Rat → Food crop → Snake → Hawk","D":"Hawk → Snake → Rat → Food crop","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Bioaccumulation","Pesticides","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The pesticide enters the food chain through plants.\nRats consume the contaminated crops.\nSnakes consume rats, and hawks consume snakes.\nPersistent pollutants become increasingly concentrated at higher trophic levels through biomagnification.","revisionPoints":"Biomagnification: concentration increases at successive trophic levels.\nTop predators are often most affected.\nPersistent pesticides can accumulate in organisms.\nFood chains transfer both energy and contaminants."},
{"no":24,"text":"Which of the following are methods of soil conservation?\nCrop rotation\nSand fences\nTerracing\nWind breaks\nSelect the correct answer.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Soil Conservation","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Crop rotation helps maintain soil fertility and reduce erosion.\nSand fences reduce wind-driven movement of soil.\nTerracing reduces runoff and erosion on slopes.\nWindbreaks reduce wind velocity and protect topsoil.","revisionPoints":"Terracing: important in hilly terrain.\nWindbreaks: reduce wind erosion.\nCrop rotation: improves soil management.\nSoil conservation aims to reduce erosion and degradation."},
{"no":25,"text":"With reference to Non-Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs), consider:\nNBFCs cannot accept demand deposits like savings accounts.\nNBFCs form part of the banking system in the same manner as commercial banks.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Non-Banking Financial Companies","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"According to the source, the first statement is treated as incorrect and the second as correct.\nNBFCs provide financial services but are distinct from ordinary commercial banks.\nThe source specifically identifies statement 2 as the correct statement.","revisionPoints":"NBFCs provide services such as:\nLoans and advances.\nInvestment activities.\nFinancing services.\nNBFCs are regulated by the RBI under applicable laws.\nNBFCs and banks are not identical institutions."},
{"no":26,"text":"Which one of the following was NOT stipulated under the Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management (FRBM) Act, 2003?","A":"Elimination of revenue deficit","B":"Reduction of fiscal deficit","C":"Elimination of primary deficit by the end of 2008–09","D":"Greater fiscal discipline and transparency","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["FRBM Act 2003","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The FRBM Act was designed to promote fiscal discipline.\nIt focused on reducing fiscal and revenue deficits.\nIt also emphasized fiscal transparency and responsible budget management.\nThe source identifies elimination of the primary deficit by the stated deadline as not being a stipulated requirement.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit: total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings.\nRevenue deficit: revenue expenditure minus revenue receipts.\nPrimary deficit: fiscal deficit minus interest payments.\nFRBM aims at sustainable fiscal management."},
{"no":27,"text":"A train travels at 60 km/h from one place towards another, while another train travels at 40 km/h in the opposite direction. If the distance between them is 150 km, after how much time will they meet?","A":"1.5 hours","B":"2 hours","C":"2.5 hours","D":"3 hours","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Speed Distance Time","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Relative speed = 60 + 40 = 100 km/h.\nDistance = 150 km.\nTime = Distance ÷ Relative speed.\nTime = 150 ÷ 100 = 1.5 hours.","revisionPoints":"Opposite-direction relative speed = sum of speeds.\nSame-direction relative speed = difference of speeds.\nFormula: Time = Distance / Speed.\nConvert units carefully in numerical questions."},
{"no":28,"text":"If there are 10 True/False questions, in how many different ways can they be answered?","A":"100","B":"512","C":"1000","D":"1024","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Permutations","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Each question has 2 possible answers: True or False.\nFor 10 independent questions, total combinations = 2¹⁰.\n2¹⁰ = 1024.","revisionPoints":"For n binary choices, combinations = 2ⁿ.\nIndependent choices are multiplied.\nSuch questions test basic principles of permutations and combinations."},
{"no":29,"text":"In stock market terminology, a person who expects the price of a share to fall is called a:","A":"Bear","B":"Bull","C":"Stag","D":"Lame duck","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Bear Market","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"A bear expects prices to decline.\nA bull expects prices to rise.\nThese terms describe market expectations and behaviour.","revisionPoints":"Bull: expects rising prices.\nBear: expects falling prices.\nBullish sentiment = optimism.\nBearish sentiment = pessimism."},
{"no":30,"text":"Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) from Mauritius into India has been significant mainly because of:","A":"Mauritius being geographically close to India","B":"India–Mauritius Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement","C":"Mauritius having a very large domestic market","D":"India having a free trade agreement with Mauritius","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["FDI","Mauritius Route","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The India–Mauritius Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) played an important role in routing investment through Mauritius.\nTax treatment under the agreement made Mauritius an important investment route.\nHence the source identifies the DTAA as the key reason.","revisionPoints":"FDI: investment giving a lasting interest/control in an enterprise.\nDTAA: agreement designed to prevent the same income from being taxed twice.\nMauritius historically became an important route for foreign investment into India.\nTax treaties can influence investment flows."},
{"no":31,"text":"A new law report is published with a red-coloured cover. Which of the following is most likely its identifying characteristic?","A":"It reports only criminal cases.","B":"It reports only constitutional cases.","C":"It is published by the Supreme Court itself.","D":"It is a new law report series identified by its publication colour.","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Books Puzzle","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The question concerns identification of a law report based on its publication characteristics.\nThe source identifies the red-coloured report as option (d).\nLaw reports compile and publish judicial decisions for legal reference.","revisionPoints":"Law reports are important sources for studying judicial decisions.\nPrecedent is an important feature of common-law legal systems.\nSupreme Court judgments can have binding precedential value under the Constitution."},
{"no":32,"text":"A crop requires a hot and humid climate, about 200 cm rainfall, and is cultivated on hill slopes up to about 1100 m. Which crop is it?","A":"Wheat","B":"Cotton","C":"Pepper","D":"Gram","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Crop Cultivation","Climate","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Black pepper requires a warm and humid tropical climate.\nIt is commonly cultivated in areas receiving high rainfall.\nIt can be grown on slopes and is associated particularly with southern India.","revisionPoints":"Black pepper: tropical spice crop.\nRequires warm, humid conditions.\nKerala and Karnataka are important producing regions.\nPepper is often grown with support trees in plantation systems."},
{"no":33,"text":"A train moving at 60 km/h takes 2 minutes to pass through a tunnel. If the tunnel is 1.5 km long, what is the length of the train?","A":"250 m","B":"500 m","C":"750 m","D":"1000 m","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Speed Distance Time","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Speed = 60 km/h = 1 km/minute.\nIn 2 minutes, train travels 2 km.\nTotal distance = train length + tunnel length.\nTrain length = 2 − 1.5 = 0.5 km = 500 m.","revisionPoints":"When a train completely crosses a tunnel:Distance = train length + tunnel length.\nConvert km/h into km/min or m/s carefully.\n60 km/h = 1 km/min = 16.67 m/s."},
{"no":34,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding neutrinos:\nThey have very small mass.\nThey have no electric charge.\nThey interact very weakly with matter.\nThey can pass through enormous amounts of matter.\nWhich of the above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Neutrino","India-based Neutrino Observatory","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Neutrinos have extremely small mass.\nThey carry no electric charge.\nTheir interaction with ordinary matter is extremely weak.\nConsequently, huge numbers of neutrinos can pass through matter with little interaction.","revisionPoints":"Neutrinos are subatomic particles.\nThey are electrically neutral.\nThey interact primarily through the weak nuclear force and gravity.\nMajor types: electron, muon and tau neutrinos."},
{"no":35,"text":"The “Instrument of Instructions” under the Government of India Act, 1935 was incorporated into the Constitution of India, 1950 as:","A":"Fundamental Rights","B":"Directive Principles of State Policy","C":"Extent of executive power of the State","D":"Fundamental Duties","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Government of India Act 1935","Instrument of Instructions","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Government of India Act, 1935 contained an Instrument of Instructions.\nThe source identifies its constitutional counterpart as provisions concerning the extent of executive power of the State.\nThis reflects the influence of the 1935 Act on the Indian Constitution.","revisionPoints":"The Constitution borrowed several administrative features from the Government of India Act, 1935.\nExamples include:\nFederal structure.\nProvincial autonomy.\nOffice of Governor.\nPublic Service Commissions.\nThe Indian Constitution also introduced several entirely new democratic features."},
{"no":36,"text":"In a hydrogen fuel cell, the product released from the cell is primarily:","A":"Hydrogen peroxide","B":"Carbon dioxide","C":"Water","D":"Oxygen","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Hydrogen Fuel Cell","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Hydrogen reacts electrochemically with oxygen in a fuel cell.\nThe principal product is water.\nElectricity and heat are also produced during the process.","revisionPoints":"Hydrogen fuel cells convert chemical energy directly into electrical energy.\nBasic reaction:Hydrogen + Oxygen → Water + Energy\nFuel cells have low local emissions.\nThey are different from conventional combustion engines."},
{"no":37,"text":"Which one of the following statements about LASIK is NOT correct?","A":"LASIK is a surgical procedure used to correct certain refractive errors.","B":"It reshapes the cornea.","C":"It can reduce dependence on spectacles or contact lenses.","D":"It can be performed on a person of any age.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["LASIK","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"LASIK is a refractive eye surgery.\nIt works by reshaping the cornea.\nPatient eligibility depends on factors including age, eye health and stability of refractive error.\nTherefore, it cannot simply be performed on a person of any age.","revisionPoints":"LASIK: Laser-Assisted in Situ Keratomileusis.\nUsed to correct certain refractive errors.\nInvolves reshaping the cornea.\nPatient selection is important before surgery."},
{"no":38,"text":"Acid rain is mainly caused by atmospheric emissions of:","A":"Carbon dioxide and methane","B":"Carbon monoxide and ozone","C":"Nitrogen oxides and sulphur oxides","D":"Hydrogen and oxygen","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Acid Rain","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Sulphur oxides (SOₓ) and nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) react with atmospheric moisture.\nThey form acids that can return to Earth through precipitation.\nThis phenomenon is known as acid rain.","revisionPoints":"SO₂ can contribute to formation of sulphuric acid.\nNOₓ contributes to nitric acid formation.\nAcid deposition can damage:\nSoil\nFreshwater ecosystems\nVegetation\nBuildings and monuments\nCoal combustion is an important source of SO₂."},
{"no":39,"text":"Six persons P, Q, R, S, T and U are seated in a row. Some face north and some face south. Based on the given conditions, which person faces north?","A":"P","B":"Q","C":"R","D":"S","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Seating Arrangement","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The question requires applying the stated seating and directional conditions.\nOn resolving the conditions, Q is the person facing north.\nSuch questions require keeping track of both position and orientation.","revisionPoints":"In seating puzzles, distinguish:\nPosition.\nRelative position.\nDirection faced.\nFor north/south-facing persons, left and right can change depending on orientation.\nSolve fixed-position clues first."},
{"no":40,"text":"Four notes of ₹1, ₹2, ₹5 and ₹10 are available. Each note can either be used or not used. How many different sums can be made using these notes?","A":"12","B":"15","C":"16","D":"18","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"There are 4 notes.\nEach note has 2 possibilities: used or not used.\nTotal combinations = 2⁴ = 16.\nOne combination uses none of the notes, giving ₹0, so it is excluded.\nTherefore, possible positive sums = 16 − 1 = 15.","revisionPoints":"Number of subsets of n items = 2ⁿ.\nExcluding the empty subset gives 2ⁿ − 1.\nHere: 2⁴ − 1 = 15.\nThis is a basic application of combinations/subsets."},
{"no":41,"text":"Which of the following are emerging economies according to UNCTAD?\nBrazil\nMexico\nSouth Africa","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["UNCTAD","Emerging Economies","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"UNCTAD classifies Brazil, Mexico and South Africa among emerging economies.\nThese countries are significant developing economies in their respective regions.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"UNCTAD = United Nations Conference on Trade and Development.\nIt focuses on trade, investment and development issues.\nEmerging economies are generally characterized by expanding economic activity and integration with global markets."},
{"no":42,"text":"Which one of the following is not related to the United Nations?","A":"International Labour Organization","B":"World Health Organization","C":"United Nations Development Programme","D":"Bank for International Settlements","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UN Related Bodies","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"ILO, WHO and UNDP are part of the UN system.\nThe Bank for International Settlements (BIS) is an independent international financial institution.\nBIS serves as a forum for central banks and promotes monetary and financial cooperation.","revisionPoints":"BIS: international organization for central banks.\nWHO: specialized UN agency for health.\nILO: specialized UN agency dealing with labour.\nUNDP: UN development organization."},
{"no":43,"text":"Consider the following terms:\nMelt down\nRecession\nSlowdown\nWhich of the above is/are correctly matched with their meanings?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Recession","Meltdown","Slowdown","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies recession with a fall in the growth rate.\nIt identifies slowdown with a fall in GDP.\n“Melt down” is associated with a sharp fall in financial/asset prices rather than the definition given in the question.\nHence statements 2 and 3 are accepted by the source.","revisionPoints":"Recession: significant decline in economic activity.\nEconomic slowdown: reduction in the pace of economic growth.\nFinancial-market terminology may differ from formal macroeconomic definitions.\nAlways distinguish growth rate from level of GDP."},
{"no":44,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding BRIC:\nBRIC originally referred to Brazil, Russia, India and China.\nThe term was coined to identify major emerging economies.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["BRIC","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"BRIC stands for Brazil, Russia, India and China.\nThe term was used to identify major emerging economies with significant economic potential.\nThe source considers both statements correct.","revisionPoints":"BRIC: Brazil + Russia + India + China.\nSouth Africa was later added, creating BRICS.\nBRICS cooperation covers areas including economics, development and international institutions."},
{"no":45,"text":"Which one of the following is an artificial international currency?","A":"Euro","B":"Yen","C":"Special Drawing Rights","D":"Pound Sterling","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["ADR","GDR","SDR","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Special Drawing Rights (SDRs) are an international reserve asset created by the IMF.\nSDRs are not a conventional currency used by individuals for daily transactions.\nTheir value is based on a basket of major currencies.","revisionPoints":"SDR issued by the IMF.\nIt is an international reserve asset.\nSDR valuation is based on a basket of currencies.\nIt can supplement member countries' official reserves."},
{"no":46,"text":"The Stiglitz Commission was concerned with:","A":"Reform of the Indian banking system","B":"Working of the global financial system and sustainable global order","C":"Agricultural reforms in developing countries","D":"Reform of the WTO dispute settlement system","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["Stiglitz Commission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The commission was established in the context of the global financial and economic crisis.\nIt examined the functioning of the international financial and economic system.\nIt also considered ways to create a more sustainable global economic order.","revisionPoints":"Associated with economist Joseph Stiglitz.\nFocused on global economic and financial governance.\nGlobal financial crises can expose weaknesses in international financial institutions.\nInternational economic governance is an important UPSC economy topic."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following mineral-resource pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Bauxite — Jharkhand","B":"Nickel — Odisha","C":"Copper — Kerala","D":"Manganese — Punjab","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Mineral Resources of India","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies Nickel–Odisha as the correctly matched pair.\nOdisha is an important mineral-producing state in India.\nThe other pairs are not correctly matched according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Odisha is rich in:\nIron ore\nBauxite\nChromite\nManganese\nNickel resources\nMineral distribution in India is strongly linked with geological formations.\nMineral-resource mapping is frequently tested in UPSC Geography."},
{"no":48,"text":"The WTO collaborates with which organization on food safety standards?","A":"Codex Alimentarius Commission","B":"International Atomic Energy Agency","C":"UN Environment Programme","D":"International Labour Organization","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["WTO","Codex Alimentarius Commission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Codex Alimentarius Commission develops international food standards.\nThese standards are relevant to food safety and international trade.\nThe WTO uses international standards such as Codex standards in the context of food-related trade disputes.","revisionPoints":"Codex Alimentarius: international food standards.\nJointly established by FAO and WHO.\nWTO deals with international trade rules.\nFood safety standards can affect international trade."},
{"no":49,"text":"The National Food Security Mission focuses on increasing production of:","A":"Rice, wheat and sugarcane","B":"Rice, wheat and pulses","C":"Wheat, cotton and pulses","D":"Rice, oilseeds and cotton","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["National Food Security Mission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The National Food Security Mission was launched to increase production of key food crops.\nThe source identifies rice, wheat and pulses as the relevant crops.\nThe programme emphasizes productivity enhancement and improved agricultural practices.","revisionPoints":"NFSM = National Food Security Mission.\nFocuses on increasing crop production and productivity.\nPulses are particularly important for nutritional and food security.\nProductivity improvement can involve improved seeds, technology and farm practices."},
{"no":50,"text":"The interest rate on savings accounts in nationalized banks was, at the time of this question, fixed by:","A":"Reserve Bank of India","B":"Ministry of Finance","C":"Individual banks","D":"None of the above","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Savings Account Interest Rate","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source gives “None of the above” as the answer.\nThe question reflects the regulatory framework existing at the time of the 2010 examination.\nBanking interest-rate regulation has changed over time, so this answer should be understood in its historical context.","revisionPoints":"Banking regulations can change through RBI policy.\nDistinguish:\nDeposit rates\nLending rates\nPolicy rates\nHistorical economy questions must be interpreted according to the period asked."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which one of the following was a recommendation of the Simon Commission?","A":"Replacement of dyarchy with responsible government in provinces","B":"Immediate grant of complete independence","C":"Abolition of provincial legislatures","D":"Introduction of universal adult franchise immediately","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Simon Commission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Simon Commission was appointed in 1927 to review constitutional reforms.\nIt recommended replacing the system of dyarchy in provinces with responsible government.\nIts arrival in India was strongly opposed because it had no Indian member.","revisionPoints":"Simon Commission: appointed 1927.\nChairman: Sir John Simon.\nIndian political parties widely boycotted it.\nSlogan: “Simon Go Back.”\nIts report influenced subsequent constitutional discussions."},
{"no":52,"text":"Which of the following was not one of the resolutions associated with the Calcutta Congress Session of 1906?","A":"Boycott","B":"Swadeshi","C":"National education","D":"Annulment of the Partition of Bengal","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Calcutta Session 1906","Surat Split 1907","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies National education as the option not included among the four resolutions being tested.\nThe 1906 Calcutta session was important in the context of the Swadeshi movement.\nThe resolutions included boycott, Swadeshi and opposition to the Partition of Bengal.","revisionPoints":"Calcutta Congress Session: 1906.\nPresident: Dadabhai Naoroji.\nSwadeshi movement followed the Partition of Bengal (1905).\nBoycott and Swadeshi became major tools of nationalist politics."},
{"no":53,"text":"If X is 80% of Z and Y is 72% of Z, then Y is what percentage less than X?","A":"8%","B":"9%","C":"10%","D":"12%","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Percentages","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Let Z = 100.\nX = 80% of 100 = 80.\nY = 72% of 100 = 72.\nDifference = 80 − 72 = 8.\nPercentage less than X = 8/80 × 100 = 10%.","revisionPoints":"Percentage comparison must use the original/reference quantity as denominator.\n“Y is what percentage less than X?” → difference ÷ X × 100.\nDo not use Z as the denominator for the final comparison."},
{"no":54,"text":"C. Rajagopalachari's “The Way Out” proposal was related to:","A":"Economic reforms","B":"Linguistic reorganization","C":"Educational reforms","D":"Solution for the constitutional deadlock","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["C. Rajagopalachari","The Way Out","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"C. Rajagopalachari proposed a formula intended to address the political and constitutional deadlock.\nIt was associated with negotiations between the Congress and Muslim League.\nThe source identifies it as a proposal for a constitutional solution.","revisionPoints":"C. Rajagopalachari: prominent Congress leader and later Governor-General of India.\nConstitutional negotiations were central to late colonial politics.\nCongress–Muslim League relations became increasingly important in the 1940s."},
{"no":55,"text":"The surviving Gupta-period cave paintings, other than those at Ajanta, are found at:","A":"Bagh","B":"Ellora","C":"Elephanta","D":"Udayagiri","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Painting","tags":["Gupta Period Paintings","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Bagh Caves in Madhya Pradesh contain important ancient paintings.\nThey are stylistically associated with the broader tradition of Indian classical mural painting.\nThe source identifies Bagh as the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Bagh Caves: Madhya Pradesh.\nAjanta Caves: Maharashtra.\nAjanta is especially famous for Buddhist murals.\nAncient Indian paintings commonly depict religious, social and cultural themes."},
{"no":56,"text":"The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) was drawn up at:","A":"Stockholm Conference, 1972","B":"Rio Earth Summit, 1992","C":"Kyoto Conference, 1997","D":"Johannesburg Summit, 2002","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UNFCCC","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The UNFCCC was opened for signature at the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.\nIt established the broad international framework for addressing climate change.\nThe Kyoto Protocol was adopted later, in 1997, under the UNFCCC.","revisionPoints":"Rio Earth Summit: 1992.\nUNFCCC = framework convention on climate change.\nKyoto Protocol: 1997.\nParis Agreement: 2015.\nUNFCCC is the foundation of international climate negotiations."},
{"no":57,"text":"Genetically manipulated natural isolates of which organism have been used for treatment of oil spills?","A":"Bacillus","B":"Rhizobium","C":"Lactobacillus","D":"Pseudomonas","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Bioremediation","Oil Spills","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Certain Pseudomonas bacteria can degrade hydrocarbons.\nSuch microorganisms can therefore be used in bioremediation.\nThe source identifies Pseudomonas as the organism associated with treatment of oil spills.","revisionPoints":"Bioremediation: use of organisms to clean polluted environments.\nPseudomonas species can degrade certain hydrocarbons.\nMicroorganisms can be useful in treating:\nOil contamination\nOrganic pollutants\nIndustrial wastes"},
{"no":58,"text":"Why are blue-green algae used as biofertilizers?","A":"They produce large quantities of oxygen.","B":"They increase soil acidity.","C":"They fix atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form.","D":"They kill harmful insects.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Blue-green Algae","Biofertilizer","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Certain blue-green algae, scientifically called cyanobacteria, can fix atmospheric nitrogen.\nNitrogen fixation converts atmospheric nitrogen into forms usable by plants.\nThis improves soil fertility and reduces dependence on synthetic nitrogen fertilizers.","revisionPoints":"Blue-green algae = cyanobacteria.\nImportant examples: Anabaena, Nostoc.\nNitrogen fixation improves soil fertility.\nParticularly associated with paddy fields."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which approach is appropriate for conservation of Ramsar sites?","A":"Complete exclusion of human activity","B":"Conversion of wetlands into agricultural land","C":"Maximum commercial exploitation","D":"Ecosystem approach with sustainable use","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Ramsar Convention","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Ramsar sites are internationally recognized wetlands of importance.\nConservation is based on maintaining ecological character while allowing appropriate sustainable use.\nThe source identifies an ecosystem approach and sustainable use as the appropriate approach.","revisionPoints":"Ramsar Convention: adopted in 1971.\nFocus: conservation and wise use of wetlands.\nRamsar site = wetland designated as being of international importance.\nSustainable use is a key principle."},
{"no":60,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Pongamia pinnata as a source of biodiesel:\nIt grows naturally in arid regions.\nIts seeds are rich in lipids.\nNearly half of its oil consists of oleic acid.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Pongamia Pinnata","Biodiesel","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Pongamia pinnata is a hardy tree that can grow under relatively dry conditions.\nIts seeds contain substantial amounts of oil.\nThe source notes a high proportion of oleic acid in its oil.\nThese characteristics make it relevant as a potential biodiesel feedstock.","revisionPoints":"Pongamia pinnata is commonly known as Karanja.\nIt is an oil-bearing tree.\nBiodiesel can be produced from plant oils through chemical processing.\nNon-edible oil sources can be considered for biofuel production."},
{"no":61,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding rice cultivation and global warming:\nRice cultivation contributes to global warming due to methane emissions from anaerobic conditions.\nRice cultivation increases atmospheric carbon dioxide through decomposition of organic matter.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Rice Cultivation","Methane","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies methane emissions from flooded rice fields as the relevant contribution to warming.\nWaterlogged rice fields create anaerobic conditions.\nUnder these conditions, methane-producing microorganisms generate methane.\nThe source marks statement 1 as correct and statement 2 as incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Paddy cultivation is an important anthropogenic source of methane.\nFlooded soils create anaerobic conditions.\nMethane is a greenhouse gas.\nAgricultural practices can influence greenhouse-gas emissions."},
{"no":62,"text":"Sustainable development is closely associated with:","A":"Maximum exploitation of natural resources","B":"Unlimited economic growth","C":"Complete elimination of industrial activity","D":"Carrying capacity of the environment","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Carrying Capacity","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Sustainable development requires development within the limits of environmental systems.\nCarrying capacity represents the level of pressure an ecosystem can sustain without long-term degradation.\nTherefore, sustainable development is closely linked with environmental carrying capacity.","revisionPoints":"Sustainable development: meeting present needs without compromising future generations.\nCarrying capacity: maximum sustainable pressure/population an environment can support.\nEconomic, social and environmental dimensions are interconnected.\nResource use must remain within ecological limits."},
{"no":63,"text":"Which one of the following is considered an energy crop for production of ethanol?","A":"Jatropha","B":"Sugarcane","C":"Cotton","D":"Groundnut","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Energy Crops","Ethanol","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies Jatropha as the answer.\nJatropha is an oil-bearing plant associated with biofuel production.\nIt has been investigated as a source of renewable energy feedstock.","revisionPoints":"Jatropha is a non-edible oilseed crop.\nIt has been investigated for biodiesel production.\nBiofuel crops can provide alternatives to fossil fuels.\nDifferent biofuels use different feedstocks."},
{"no":64,"text":"Which of the following protected area–species pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Bhitarkanika — Saltwater crocodile","B":"Desert National Park — Great Indian Bustard","C":"Eravikulam National Park — Hoolock gibbon","D":"All of the above","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Protected Areas of India","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Bhitarkanika is well known for its population of saltwater crocodiles.\nThe Great Indian Bustard is associated with the arid and semi-arid habitats of western India, but the source does not accept the Desert National Park pairing in this question.\nHoolock gibbons are associated with northeastern India, not Eravikulam.","revisionPoints":"Bhitarkanika: Odisha.\nImportant for saltwater crocodiles and mangrove ecosystems.\nGreat Indian Bustard: grassland/arid habitats.\nHoolock gibbon: northeastern India."},
{"no":65,"text":"Why are some plants insectivorous?","A":"They require insects for pollination","B":"They grow in nitrogen-deficient soils","C":"They cannot perform photosynthesis","D":"They depend entirely on insects for energy","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Insectivorous Plants","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Insectivorous plants can perform photosynthesis.\nThey generally grow in soils that are poor in nitrogen.\nThey obtain nitrogen and other nutrients by trapping and digesting insects.\nTheir insect-feeding habit supplements their mineral nutrition.","revisionPoints":"Insectivorous plants are photosynthetic.\nInsects mainly provide nutrients, especially nitrogen.\nExamples:\nPitcher plant\nVenus flytrap\nSundew\nThey are often found in nutrient-poor habitats."},
{"no":66,"text":"A person has to travel 50 km. He walks at 4 km/h and cycles at 10 km/h. If the total time taken is 8 hours, how much distance did he cover on foot?","A":"15 km","B":"20 km","C":"25 km","D":"30 km","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Speed Distance Time","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Let distance walked = x km.\nDistance cycled = 50 − x km.\nTotal time:x/4 + (50 − x)/10 = 8\nSolving gives x = 20 km.","revisionPoints":"For combined-speed problems, calculate time separately for each mode.\nFormula: Time = Distance / Speed.\nTotal distance = sum of distances covered by each mode.\nConvert units consistently."},
{"no":67,"text":"Which of the following measures can help control fiscal deficit?\nEncouraging FDI inflows\nPrivatization of higher education institutions\nDownsizing bureaucracy","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Fiscal Deficit","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies all three measures as possible ways to improve fiscal conditions.\nFDI can provide additional capital inflows.\nPrivatization can reduce certain government expenditure responsibilities.\nDownsizing bureaucracy can reduce administrative expenditure.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit reflects the government's borrowing requirement.\nDeficit can be reduced through:\nHigher revenues.\nLower expenditure.\nImproved efficiency.\nFDI is a capital inflow, not government revenue itself."},
{"no":68,"text":"Which region experienced the fastest urbanization growth during the last three decades according to the source?","A":"Asia","B":"Europe","C":"North America","D":"Australia","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UN-Habitat","Urbanization","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies Asia as having the fastest urbanization growth during the stated period.\nRapid urbanization is associated with industrialization, employment opportunities and migration.\nAsian cities have experienced substantial population growth and expansion.","revisionPoints":"Urbanization: increasing share of population living in urban areas.\nMajor drivers:\nEmployment.\nIndustrialization.\nBetter services.\nRural-to-urban migration.\nRapid urbanization creates infrastructure and environmental challenges."},
{"no":69,"text":"Which is the largest forest type in India by area?","A":"Tropical Dry Deciduous Forest","B":"Tropical Evergreen Forest","C":"Tropical Moist Deciduous Forest","D":"Montane Forest","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Forest Types of India","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies Tropical Moist Deciduous Forest as the largest forest type by area.\nThese forests occur in regions receiving moderate to high rainfall.\nTrees generally shed leaves during the dry season.","revisionPoints":"Deciduous forests shed leaves during the dry season.\nMoist deciduous forests are widespread in India.\nImportant species can include sal and teak.\nForest distribution depends strongly on rainfall and temperature."},
{"no":70,"text":"Which one of the following was NOT included as an objective of inclusive growth under the Eleventh Five Year Plan?","A":"Reduction of poverty","B":"Creation of employment opportunities","C":"Strengthening the capital market","D":"Improvement in education and health","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Inclusive Growth","11th Five-Year Plan","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Eleventh Plan emphasized inclusive growth.\nMajor concerns included poverty reduction, employment, education and health.\nStrengthening the capital market was not identified as a core inclusive-growth objective in the source.","revisionPoints":"Eleventh Five Year Plan: 2007–2012.\nTheme: “Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth.”\nFocus areas included:\nPoverty reduction.\nEmployment.\nEducation.\nHealth.\nInclusive growth emphasizes participation of disadvantaged groups."},
{"no":71,"text":"How many numbers between 0 and 999 are not divisible by either 5 or 7?","A":"680","B":"684","C":"686","D":"688","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Divisibility","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Numbers from 0 to 999 give 1000 numbers.\nMultiples of 5 = 200.\nMultiples of 7 = 143.\nMultiples of both 5 and 7 = multiples of 35 = 29.\nNumbers divisible by 5 or 7 = 200 + 143 − 29 = 314.\nTherefore, numbers not divisible by either = 1000 − 314 = 686.","revisionPoints":"Use inclusion–exclusion principle when two divisibility conditions overlap.\nFormula:n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B)\nLCM of 5 and 7 = 35."},
{"no":72,"text":"Tamil Nadu has been an important producer of mill-made cotton yarn mainly because of:","A":"Large cotton plantations and abundant coal","B":"Extensive irrigation and mineral resources","C":"Skilled labour and availability of black cotton soil","D":"High rainfall and large forest resources","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Cotton Yarn","Tamil Nadu","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies skilled labour and black cotton soil as the relevant factors.\nBlack cotton soil supports cotton cultivation.\nSkilled labour supports textile and spinning industries.","revisionPoints":"Cotton textile industry depends on:\nRaw material.\nLabour.\nPower.\nTransport.\nMarkets.\nBlack soil is particularly suitable for cotton.\nTamil Nadu is an important textile-manufacturing state."},
{"no":73,"text":"Consider the following statements about freshwater:\nLess than 1% of the Earth's freshwater is usable by humans.\nAbout 95% of freshwater is found in polar ice and glaciers.\nWhich is/are correct according to the source?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Freshwater Resources","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as incorrect.\nThe distribution of freshwater includes glaciers and ice caps, groundwater, surface water and other forms.\nThe exact proportions depend on the classification and definition of usable freshwater.","revisionPoints":"Most of Earth's water is saltwater.\nFreshwater is a very small fraction of total global water.\nMajor freshwater stores include:\nGlaciers and ice caps.\nGroundwater.\nSurface water.\nAccessible freshwater is much smaller than total freshwater."},
{"no":74,"text":"Fresh snow reflects most of the incoming sunlight because:","A":"Snow absorbs most solar radiation","B":"Snow has very low albedo","C":"Snow has high albedo","D":"Snow contains large quantities of water vapour","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Albedo","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Albedo is the fraction of incoming radiation reflected by a surface.\nFresh snow has a very high albedo.\nTherefore, it reflects a large proportion of incoming sunlight.","revisionPoints":"High albedo: more reflection.\nLow albedo: more absorption.\nFresh snow is highly reflective.\nMelting snow reduces surface albedo and can reinforce warming."},
{"no":75,"text":"Which of the following rivers flow through Himachal Pradesh?\nBeas\nChenab\nRavi\nSutlej\nYamuna","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1, 3, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Rivers of Himachal Pradesh","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies all five rivers as flowing through Himachal Pradesh.\nBeas, Chenab, Ravi and Sutlej are major Himalayan river systems.\nThe Yamuna also has its source in the Himalayan region and flows through Himachal Pradesh before entering the plains.","revisionPoints":"Beas: originates near Rohtang region.\nSutlej: enters India through Himachal Pradesh.\nChenab: formed by the confluence of Chandra and Bhaga.\nRavi: important river of Himachal Pradesh and Punjab.\nYamuna: originates from Yamunotri glacier."},
{"no":76,"text":"Who lays the recommendations of the Finance Commission before Parliament?","A":"The President of India","B":"The Prime Minister","C":"The Finance Minister","D":"The Speaker of Lok Sabha","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Finance Commission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Finance Commission is constituted under Article 280.\nIts recommendations are submitted to the President.\nThe President causes the recommendations, along with an explanatory memorandum on action taken, to be laid before Parliament.","revisionPoints":"Article 280: Finance Commission.\nConstituted by the President.\nRecommends distribution of financial resources between Union and States.\nIt is a constitutional body."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which department prepares and presents the Union Budget to Parliament?","A":"Department of Revenue","B":"Department of Economic Affairs","C":"Department of Expenditure","D":"NITI Aayog","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Union Budget","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies the Department of Economic Affairs (DEA) as responsible for preparing and presenting the Union Budget.\nThe Budget process involves several departments of the Ministry of Finance.\nDEA has a central role in the budgetary process.","revisionPoints":"Union Budget is constitutionally linked with the Annual Financial Statement.\nMinistry of Finance plays the central role.\nMajor departments include:\nEconomic Affairs.\nExpenditure.\nRevenue.\nParliament exercises control over public finances."},
{"no":78,"text":"In a group of persons, a professor, doctor, engineer and lawyer are present. Based on the given clues, who is the professor?","A":"A","B":"C","C":"D","D":"E","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Family Puzzle","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The question is a logical arrangement problem.\nApplying the stated clues and eliminating incompatible possibilities identifies C as the professor.\nSuch questions require combining all clues rather than considering them individually.","revisionPoints":"Logical reasoning questions often use elimination.\nConvert each clue into a definite restriction.\nAvoid assuming information not provided.\nCheck the final arrangement against every condition."},
{"no":79,"text":"Which of the following measures constitutes fiscal stimulus during a recession?\nCutting tax rates\nIncreasing government expenditure","A":"Both 1 and 2","B":"1 only","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Fiscal Stimulus","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Fiscal stimulus uses government fiscal policy to increase aggregate demand.\nCutting taxes can increase disposable income and consumption.\nIncreasing government expenditure directly adds to aggregate demand.\nTherefore, both are fiscal-stimulus measures.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal policy: government taxation and expenditure.\nExpansionary fiscal policy includes:\nLower taxes.\nHigher government spending.\nIt is commonly used to support demand during economic slowdown/recession.\nMonetary policy is conducted primarily through the central bank."},
{"no":80,"text":"In a village, some people are literate and some are illiterate. Which of the following conclusions is certainly true based on the given information?","A":"All villagers below 25 years are literate.","B":"Some villagers below 25 years are literate.","C":"All villagers above 25 years are illiterate.","D":"No villager below 25 years is literate.","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Villagers Puzzle","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source's logical conditions lead to the conclusion that some villagers below 25 years are literate.\nThe other statements make absolute claims that are not supported.\nIn logical reasoning, a conclusion must follow necessarily from the given conditions.","revisionPoints":"Distinguish “some” from “all” and “none.”\nA universal statement requires evidence covering the entire group.\nLogical conclusions must follow from the given premises only.\nAvoid introducing assumptions."},
{"no":81,"text":"An increase in the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) would have which of the following effects?","A":"Commercial banks will have less money to lend","B":"Commercial banks will have more money to lend","C":"Money supply will necessarily increase","D":"Interest rates will necessarily fall","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Cash Reserve Ratio","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"CRR is the proportion of deposits that commercial banks must maintain with the RBI.\nWhen CRR increases, banks have to keep a larger portion of their deposits as reserves.\nThis reduces the funds available for lending.\nTherefore, the source identifies option (a) as correct.","revisionPoints":"CRR: Cash Reserve Ratio.\nHigher CRR → lower lendable funds.\nLower CRR → greater lendable funds.\nCRR is a monetary policy instrument."},
{"no":82,"text":"Who created the Covenanted Civil Service, which later evolved into the Indian Civil Service?","A":"Warren Hastings","B":"William Bentinck","C":"Lord Cornwallis","D":"Lord Curzon","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Covenanted Civil Service","Cornwallis","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Lord Cornwallis introduced important administrative and civil-service reforms.\nThe Covenanted Civil Service became the foundation for the later Indian Civil Service.\nCornwallis sought to establish a more organized and professional administrative structure.","revisionPoints":"Lord Cornwallis: Governor-General, 1786–1793.\nAssociated with:\nPermanent Settlement.\nCivil service reforms.\nJudicial reforms.\nCovenanted Civil Service was dominated by Europeans during the colonial period.\nLater evolved into the Indian Civil Service (ICS)."},
{"no":83,"text":"What was the immediate cause of the Swadeshi Movement?","A":"Partition of Bengal by Lord Curzon","B":"Rowlatt Act","C":"Jallianwala Bagh massacre","D":"Simon Commission","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Swadeshi Movement","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Lord Curzon partitioned Bengal in 1905.\nThe partition generated widespread opposition among Indian nationalists.\nThe resulting boycott of foreign goods and promotion of indigenous products became central to the Swadeshi Movement.","revisionPoints":"Partition of Bengal: 1905.\nViceroy: Lord Curzon.\nSwadeshi = promotion of indigenous goods.\nBoycott of foreign goods was a major method.\nThe partition was annulled in 1911."},
{"no":84,"text":"With reference to the Champaran Satyagraha, consider the following:\nMahatma Gandhi led the movement against the indigo plantation system.\nJ. B. Kripalani was associated with Gandhi during the movement.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Champaran Satyagraha","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"According to the source's answer key, only statement 2 is accepted as correct.\nJ. B. Kripalani was associated with Gandhi during the Champaran movement.\nThe source does not accept statement 1 in the form presented.","revisionPoints":"Champaran Satyagraha: 1917.\nLocated in present-day Bihar.\nConnected with indigo cultivation and the problems faced by cultivators.\nIt was among Gandhi's important early movements in India."},
{"no":85,"text":"Why did Cornwallis remove the judicial powers of the District Collector in 1793?","A":"The Collector was corrupt","B":"The Collector had no administrative experience","C":"The judiciary was intended to be completely independent of the executive","D":"Judicial work required specialized legal knowledge and training","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["District Collector","Cornwallis 1793","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Cornwallis believed judicial functions required specialized knowledge of law.\nThe Collector was primarily responsible for revenue administration.\nJudicial responsibilities were therefore separated from the Collector's functions.\nThe source identifies specialized legal knowledge as the reason.","revisionPoints":"Cornwallis reforms: important for judicial and administrative organization.\nSeparation of judicial and revenue functions was emphasized.\nDistrict Collector became primarily associated with revenue administration.\nCornwallis is often associated with the early institutionalization of British administration."},
{"no":86,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Wholesale Price Index (WPI):\nWPI is published only once a month.\nWPI gives less weight to food articles than CPI (Industrial Workers).\nWhich is/are correct according to the source?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["WPI","CPI","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as incorrect.\nThe question reflects the statistical and inflation-measurement framework applicable at the time.\nWPI and CPI measure price changes using different baskets and methodologies.","revisionPoints":"WPI: Wholesale Price Index.\nCPI: Consumer Price Index.\nWPI broadly tracks wholesale-level price movements.\nCPI is more directly related to prices experienced by consumers.\nInflation questions should always consider the base year and index methodology."},
{"no":87,"text":"In a ranking competition involving 11 persons, every person competes with every other person exactly once. How many comparisons are required?","A":"44","B":"55","C":"66","D":"110","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Every pair of persons has one comparison.\nNumber of pairs among 11 persons:11 × 10 / 2 = 55\nTherefore, 55 comparisons are required.","revisionPoints":"Number of pairs among n objects = n(n−1)/2.\nFor 11:11 × 10 / 2 = 55.\nThis is a standard combination formula:¹¹C₂ = 55."},
{"no":88,"text":"The radiator of a motor car works mainly on the principle of:","A":"Conduction","B":"Convection","C":"Radiation","D":"Evaporation","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Convection","Radiator","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The engine transfers heat to the coolant.\nThe heated coolant circulates through the radiator.\nHeat is transferred from the coolant to the surrounding air.\nThe source identifies convection as the principal mechanism.","revisionPoints":"Conduction: heat transfer through direct contact.\nConvection: heat transfer through movement of fluids.\nRadiation: heat transfer through electromagnetic waves.\nVehicle cooling systems use fluid circulation and heat exchange."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which one of the following does not belong to the GSM family of mobile communication technologies?","A":"EDGE","B":"LTE","C":"DSL","D":"GSM","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["GSM","Wireless Technologies","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"GSM is a cellular mobile communication standard.\nEDGE is an enhancement associated with GSM networks.\nLTE is part of the evolution of mobile cellular technologies.\nDSL is a fixed broadband technology and does not belong to the GSM family.","revisionPoints":"GSM: Global System for Mobile Communications.\nEDGE: enhanced mobile data technology.\nLTE: Long-Term Evolution.\nDSL: Digital Subscriber Line, primarily fixed-line broadband."},
{"no":90,"text":"Which one of the following statements about CyberKnife is NOT correct?","A":"It is a robotic radiation therapy system.","B":"It can deliver focused radiation to tumors.","C":"It can be used for certain tumors that are difficult to treat conventionally.","D":"It can map the spread of a tumor throughout the body.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Cyberknife","Cancer Treatment","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"CyberKnife is a robotic stereotactic radiation therapy system.\nIt delivers highly focused radiation to targeted tumors.\nIts function is treatment rather than mapping the entire spread of a tumor throughout the body.\nTherefore, option (d) is the incorrect statement according to the source.","revisionPoints":"CyberKnife uses precisely targeted radiation.\nIt is a form of stereotactic radiosurgery/radiotherapy.\nIt can be used for selected tumors.\nTreatment technology should not be confused with diagnostic imaging."},
{"no":91,"text":"Red soil in India is mainly red because of the presence of:","A":"Calcium carbonate","B":"Magnesium","C":"Ferric oxides","D":"Humus","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Red Soil","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Red colour in soil is primarily associated with iron compounds.\nFerric oxides give the soil its characteristic red or reddish-brown appearance.\nRed soils are widely distributed in peninsular India.","revisionPoints":"Red soil: associated with iron oxide.\nOften develops from crystalline and metamorphic rocks.\nGenerally low in nitrogen, phosphorus and humus.\nCommon in parts of southern and eastern India."},
{"no":92,"text":"Why are Gondwana rocks important in India?","A":"They contain most of India's petroleum reserves.","B":"More than 90% of India's coal reserves are found in them.","C":"They contain India's largest iron-ore deposits.","D":"They are the main source of alluvial soil.","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Gondwana Rocks","Coal","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Gondwana formations contain major coal-bearing strata.\nThe source states that more than 90% of India's coal reserves occur in these formations.\nMajor coalfields of eastern and central India are associated with Gondwana rocks.","revisionPoints":"Gondwana rocks: important for coal.\nMajor coalfields occur in:\nJharkhand\nOdisha\nChhattisgarh\nWest Bengal\nMadhya Pradesh\nCoal is an important fossil fuel resource."},
{"no":93,"text":"The Strait of Malacca connects the:","A":"Arabian Sea and Red Sea","B":"Bay of Bengal and South China Sea","C":"Pacific Ocean and Atlantic Ocean","D":"Indian Ocean and Mediterranean Sea","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Strait of Malacca","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Strait of Malacca lies between the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra.\nIt connects the Indian Ocean side, through the Andaman Sea/Bay of Bengal, with the South China Sea.\nIt is one of the world's most important maritime trade routes.","revisionPoints":"Strait of Malacca: major maritime chokepoint.\nLies between:\nMalay Peninsula\nSumatra\nSingapore lies at the eastern end of the strait.\nImportant for trade between the Indian and Pacific Ocean regions."},
{"no":94,"text":"The Luni River finally disappears into the:","A":"Arabian Sea","B":"Gulf of Khambhat","C":"Sambhar Lake","D":"Rann of Kutch","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["River Luni","Rann of Kutch","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Luni is an important river of Rajasthan.\nIt flows through the arid region of western Rajasthan.\nIts waters eventually disappear into the marshy region of the Rann of Kutch.","revisionPoints":"Luni is associated with western Rajasthan.\nIt is an important river of the arid region.\nIts lower course is affected by the desert environment.\nRann of Kutch: extensive salt-marsh region in Gujarat."},
{"no":95,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is incorrectly matched?","A":"Wular Lake — Jhelum","B":"Kolleru Lake — Krishna","C":"Chilika Lake — Daya","D":"Pulicat Lake — Arani","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Dams and Lakes of India","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies Kolleru Lake–Krishna as the incorrect pairing.\nKolleru is a major freshwater lake in Andhra Pradesh.\nIt lies between the Krishna and Godavari river deltas, rather than being directly identified with the Krishna as its river pairing in the source.","revisionPoints":"Kolleru Lake: Andhra Pradesh.\nLocated between the Krishna and Godavari deltas.\nChilika: Odisha, coastal brackish-water lagoon.\nWular: Jammu and Kashmir, associated with the Jhelum basin."},
{"no":96,"text":"A region has warm, dry summers and mild, wet winters. Evergreen oak vegetation is found there. Which climate is characteristic of this region?","A":"Mediterranean climate","B":"Equatorial climate","C":"Tropical monsoon climate","D":"Tundra climate","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Mediterranean Climate","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Mediterranean climate is characterized by:\nHot/dry summers\nMild/wet winters\nEvergreen vegetation is adapted to seasonal dryness.\nThe source identifies the Mediterranean climate.","revisionPoints":"Mediterranean climate occurs roughly between 30°–45° latitude on western continental margins.\nRainfall mainly occurs in winter.\nTypical vegetation includes sclerophyllous evergreen vegetation.\nImportant agricultural products include olives, grapes and citrus fruits."},
{"no":97,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the National Rehabilitation and Resettlement Policy, 2007:\nIt applies only to people displaced by government projects.\nIt provides no provisions relating to rehabilitation of displaced families.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["National Rehabilitation and Resettlement Policy 2007","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as incorrect.\nThe policy addressed rehabilitation and resettlement of people affected by development projects.\nIt was intended to provide a framework for addressing displacement and rehabilitation concerns.","revisionPoints":"Rehabilitation: restoring livelihoods and living conditions.\nResettlement: relocation of affected people.\nDevelopment projects can cause displacement.\nRehabilitation policies aim to reduce the social and economic consequences of displacement."},
{"no":98,"text":"The major shift away from heavy industries towards infrastructure and other sectors began during which Five Year Plan?","A":"Third Plan","B":"Fifth Plan","C":"Eighth Plan","D":"Tenth Plan","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Five-Year Plans","Industrialization","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies the Eighth Five Year Plan as the period when the shift began.\nThe Eighth Plan operated during 1992–1997.\nIt followed the economic reforms initiated in 1991.","revisionPoints":"Eighth Five Year Plan: 1992–1997.\nFollowed the beginning of economic liberalization in 1991.\nGreater emphasis was placed on:\nInfrastructure.\nHuman development.\nEconomic reforms.\nIt marked an important transition in India's planning approach."},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Swadhar and Swayam Siddha:\nSwadhar was aimed at women in difficult circumstances.\nSwayam Siddha focused on empowering women through self-help groups and collective action.\nWhich is/are correct according to the source?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Swadhar","Swayam Siddha","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source's answer key identifies both statements as incorrect.\nThe question is based on the specific definitions and scope of the schemes as understood in the source.\nTherefore, the answer is neither 1 nor 2 according to the provided source.","revisionPoints":"Swadhar: associated with support for women in difficult circumstances.\nSwayam Siddha: associated with women's empowerment.\nGovernment schemes should be studied through their:\nTarget group.\nObjectives.\nImplementing agency.\nKey components."},
{"no":100,"text":"Which of the following are recognized as rights of children under the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child (UNCRC)?\nRight to development\nRight to expression\nRight to recreation","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UN Convention on the Rights of the Child","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The UNCRC recognizes a broad range of rights for children.\nThese include rights related to development, freedom of expression and recreation.\nTherefore, all three are recognized rights according to the source.","revisionPoints":"UNCRC: United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child.\nAdopted by the UN General Assembly in 1989.\nCovers:\nSurvival.\nDevelopment.\nProtection.\nParticipation.\nChildren have rights to education, health, protection and participation."},
{"no":101,"text":"Three men start together to travel the same way around a circular track of 11 km. Their speeds are 4, 5.5 and 8 km/h respectively. When will they meet at the starting point for the first time?","A":"After 11 hours","B":"After 21 hours","C":"After 22 hours","D":"After 33 hours","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Circular Track","LCM","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"They will meet at the starting point when each completes a whole number of rounds.\nTime for one round:\nFirst man = 11/4 hours\nSecond man = 11/5.5 = 2 hours\nThird man = 11/8 hours\nWe need the least common time at which all three are at the starting point.\nThe required time is 22 hours.","revisionPoints":"Circular-track problems require finding a common multiple of lap times.\nDistance = Speed × Time.\nFor repeated meetings, identify the least common multiple/common time."},
{"no":102,"text":"With reference to the Consumer Disputes Redressal at district level in India, which one of the following statements is not correct?","A":"A State Government can establish more than one District Forum in a district if it deems fit.","B":"One of the members of the District Forum shall be a woman.","C":"The District Forum entertains complaints where the value of goods or services does not exceed ₹50 lakh.","D":"A complaint may be filed by the State Government as a representative of the interests of consumers in general.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["Consumer Disputes Redressal","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies statement (c) as incorrect.\nThe monetary jurisdiction mentioned in the question reflects the historical consumer-protection framework applicable at the time.\nConsumer dispute institutions have undergone subsequent legislative changes.","revisionPoints":"Consumer protection involves institutional mechanisms for grievance redressal.\nDistrict-level consumer forums/commissions deal with consumer disputes within their jurisdiction.\nAlways consider the law applicable during the period of the question."},
{"no":103,"text":"King Cobra is the only snake that makes its own nest. Why does it make its nest?","A":"It is a snake-eater and the nest helps attract other snakes.","B":"It is a viviparous snake and needs a nest to give birth to its offspring.","C":"It is an oviparous snake and lays its eggs in the nest and guards the nest until they are hatched.","D":"It is a large, cold-blooded animal and needs a nest to hibernate.","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["King Cobra","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"King Cobra is oviparous, meaning it lays eggs.\nIt constructs a nest using vegetation.\nThe female guards the nest and eggs during incubation.\nThis nesting behaviour is unusual among snakes.","revisionPoints":"Oviparous: reproduces by laying eggs.\nKing Cobra = Ophiophagus hannah.\nIt is the world's longest venomous snake.\nThe female guards the nest during incubation."},
{"no":104,"text":"As a result of their annual survey, the National Geographic Society and GlobeScan gave India top rank in the Greendex 2009 score. What is this score?","A":"A measure of efforts made by countries to adopt technologies for reducing carbon footprint","B":"A measure of environmentally sustainable consumer behaviour in different countries","C":"An assessment of programmes for improving conservation of natural resources","D":"An index showing the volume of carbon credits sold by different countries","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Greendex 2009","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Greendex measures environmentally sustainable consumer behaviour.\nIt evaluates how consumers in different countries behave in areas such as energy, transportation, food and consumption.\nIt is therefore different from a carbon-credit or government-policy index.","revisionPoints":"Greendex relates to consumer behaviour and sustainability.\nSustainable consumption aims to reduce environmental impact.\nIt is different from:\nCarbon trading.\nCarbon credits.\nGovernment conservation programmes."},
{"no":105,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Taxus tree is naturally found in the Himalayas.\nThe Taxus tree is listed in the Red Data Book.\nA drug called Taxol obtained from Taxus is effective against Parkinson's disease.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Taxus Tree","Taxol","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Taxus species are naturally found in Himalayan regions.\nTaxus species have conservation concerns and are included in threatened-species assessments.\nThe source does not accept the claim that Taxol is effective against Parkinson's disease.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Taxus is commonly known as yew.\nTaxol/paclitaxel is an important anti-cancer drug.\nHimalayan Taxus species face conservation pressures.\nMedicinal plants can have both ecological and pharmaceutical importance."},
{"no":106,"text":"P, Q, R and S are four men.\nP is the oldest but not the poorest.\nR is the richest but not the oldest.\nQ is older than S but not older than P or R.\nP is richer than Q but not richer than S.\nThe four men can be ordered, in descending order, with respect to age and richness respectively as:","A":"PQRS, RPSQ","B":"PRQS, RSPQ","C":"PRQS, RSQP","D":"PRSQ, RSPQ","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Age and Richness Puzzle","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"From age:\nP is oldest.\nR is younger than P.\nQ is younger than P and R but older than S.\nTherefore: P > R > Q > S.\nFrom richness:\nR is richest.\nP is richer than Q but poorer than S.\nTherefore: R > S > P > Q.\nHence the correct ordering is PRQS, RSPQ.","revisionPoints":"Translate each statement into a ranking relationship.\nSeparate different criteria such as age and wealth.\nSolve each ranking independently before combining them."},
{"no":107,"text":"What causes wind to deflect towards the left in the Southern Hemisphere?","A":"Temperature","B":"Magnetic field","C":"Rotation of the Earth","D":"Pressure","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Coriolis Force","Wind Deflection","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Earth's rotation produces the Coriolis effect.\nMoving air is deflected to the:\nRight in the Northern Hemisphere.\nLeft in the Southern Hemisphere.\nTherefore, Earth's rotation is the underlying cause.","revisionPoints":"Coriolis effect results from Earth's rotation.\nNorthern Hemisphere → deflection to the right.\nSouthern Hemisphere → deflection to the left.\nCoriolis effect is zero at the Equator and increases towards the poles."},
{"no":108,"text":"Indiscriminate disposal of used fluorescent lamps causes mercury pollution. Why is mercury used in these lamps?","A":"Mercury coating makes the light bright white.","B":"When switched on, mercury causes emission of ultraviolet radiation.","C":"Mercury converts ultraviolet energy into visible light.","D":"None of the above.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Mercury Pollution","Fluorescent Lamps","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Electrical discharge through mercury vapour produces ultraviolet radiation.\nThe UV radiation interacts with the phosphor coating inside the fluorescent lamp.\nThe phosphor then produces visible light.\nTherefore, mercury plays a key role in the production of UV radiation.","revisionPoints":"Fluorescent lamps contain mercury vapour.\nMercury discharge produces UV radiation.\nPhosphor coating converts UV into visible light.\nImproper disposal can release mercury into the environment."},
{"no":109,"text":"If there were no Himalayan ranges, what would have been the most likely geographical impact on India?\nMuch of the country would experience cold waves from Siberia.\nIndo-Gangetic Plain would be devoid of such extensive alluvial soils.\nThe pattern of monsoon would be different from what it is at present.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Himalayas","Monsoon","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Himalayas act as a major climatic barrier against cold continental air from Central Asia.\nHimalayan rivers contribute enormous quantities of sediment to the Indo-Gangetic plains.\nThe Himalayan system also plays an important role in the Indian monsoon circulation.\nTherefore, all three consequences are identified as correct by the source.","revisionPoints":"Himalayas act as a climatic barrier.\nThey contribute to the formation of extensive alluvial plains.\nHimalayan topography influences the Indian monsoon.\nMajor Himalayan rivers include the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra systems."},
{"no":110,"text":"In the context of space technology, what is Bhuvan?","A":"A mini satellite launched by ISRO for distance education","B":"The name given to the next Moon Impact Probe for Chandrayaan-II","C":"A geoportal of ISRO with 3D imaging capabilities of India","D":"A space telescope developed by India","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Bhuvan","ISRO Geoportal","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Bhuvan is a geospatial platform developed by ISRO.\nIt provides satellite-based geographic information and visualization.\nIt includes mapping and visualization capabilities, including 3D representations.","revisionPoints":"Bhuvan is associated with ISRO.\nIt is a geoportal.\nUses satellite imagery and geospatial technologies.\nApplications include mapping, planning and disaster-related information."},
{"no":111,"text":"The latitudes that pass through Sikkim also pass through:","A":"Rajasthan","B":"Punjab","C":"Himachal Pradesh","D":"Jammu & Kashmir","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Latitude","Sikkim","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Sikkim and parts of Rajasthan share certain latitude ranges.\nThe question tests understanding of India's east–west geographical extent.\nAccording to the source, the correct state is Rajasthan.","revisionPoints":"India extends approximately from 8°4′N to 37°6′N.\nLatitude influences:\nTemperature.\nDay length.\nClimatic conditions.\nLongitudes are more directly related to differences in local time."},
{"no":112,"text":"A man fills a basket with eggs such that the number of eggs added each day is equal to the number already present. The basket becomes completely full in 24 days. After how many days was it one-fourth full?","A":"6","B":"12","C":"17","D":"22","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Geometric Progression","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Each day, the number of eggs doubles.\nIf the basket is full on day 24:\nDay 23 → half full.\nDay 22 → one-fourth full.\nTherefore, it was one-fourth full after 22 days.","revisionPoints":"This is an exponential growth problem.\nIf quantity doubles daily:\nOne day before full → half full.\nTwo days before full → quarter full.\nWork backwards for faster solving."},
{"no":113,"text":"The diameters of two circular coins are in the ratio 1:3. The smaller coin is made to roll around the bigger coin until it returns to its starting position. How many times does the smaller coin rotate around its own centre?","A":"9","B":"6","C":"3","D":"1.5","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Circles","Ratio","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Let the smaller coin's radius be r.\nThe larger coin's radius is 3r.\nWhen a circle rolls externally around another circle, the number of rotations depends on the ratio of the sum of radii to the rolling coin's radius.\nHere:(3r + r)/r = 4, but the source's answer key identifies 3 based on its intended interpretation of the rolling geometry.","revisionPoints":"Rolling-circle problems require careful distinction between:\nRotation around its own centre.\nRevolution around the other circle.\nCircumference = 2πr.\nRatio of diameters = ratio of radii."},
{"no":114,"text":"The difference between the simple interest received from two banks on ₹500 for two years is ₹2.50. What is the difference between their rates?","A":"0.25%","B":"0.5%","C":"1%","D":"2.5%","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Simple Interest","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Simple Interest:SI = P × R × T / 100\nDifference in SI = ₹2.50.\nTherefore:2.50 = 500 × R × 2 / 100\nR = 0.25%.","revisionPoints":"Simple Interest formula:SI = PRT/100\nPrincipal = ₹500.\nTime = 2 years.\nThe question asks for the difference in rates, not the actual individual rates."},
{"no":115,"text":"When ten persons shake hands with one another, how many handshakes are possible?","A":"20","B":"25","C":"40","D":"45","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","Handshakes","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Each handshake occurs between a unique pair of people.\nNumber of pairs among 10 people:10 × 9 / 2 = 45.\nTherefore, 45 handshakes are possible.","revisionPoints":"Formula for pairwise interactions:n(n−1)/2\nFor 10 people:10C2 = 45.\nEach pair shakes hands only once."},
{"no":116,"text":"A candidate attempted 12 questions and secured full marks in all of them. If he obtained 60% in the test and all questions carried equal marks, what is the total number of questions?","A":"36","B":"30","C":"25","D":"20","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Percentages","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The candidate obtained full marks in 12 questions.\nThese 12 questions represent 60% of the total marks.\nTherefore:12 ÷ 0.60 = 20\nHence the test contained 20 questions.","revisionPoints":"Equal marks per question means marks are proportional to the number of questions.\nFormula:Total = Obtained portion ÷ Percentage\n12 = 60% → Total = 20."},
{"no":117,"text":"In how many ways can four children be made to stand in a line such that two of them, A and B, are always together?","A":"6","B":"12","C":"18","D":"24","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Permutations","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Treat A and B as one combined block.\nThen there are 3 units to arrange:\nAB\nC\nD\nThese can be arranged in 3! = 6 ways.\nA and B can switch positions within the block in 2! = 2 ways.\nTotal = 6 × 2 = 12.","revisionPoints":"“Always together” → treat the group as a single block.\nNumber of arrangements:(n−1)! × internal arrangements\nHere:3! × 2! = 12."},
{"no":118,"text":"In a meeting, a map of a village was placed in such a manner that south-east becomes north, north-east becomes west and so on. What will south become?","A":"North","B":"North-east","C":"North-west","D":"West","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Directions","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The map has been rotated so that south-east is represented as north.\nThis establishes a fixed rotational transformation.\nApplying the same transformation to south gives north-east.\nHence the source identifies option (b).","revisionPoints":"Direction questions require identifying the rotation/transformation first.\nStandard directions:\nNorth\nSouth\nEast\nWest\nNE, NW, SE, SW\nApply the same rotation consistently to every direction."},
{"no":119,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe “Bombay Manifesto” signed in 1936 openly opposed the preaching of socialist ideals.\nIt evoked support from a large section of the business community across India.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Bombay Manifesto 1936","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Bombay Manifesto of 1936 was associated with opposition to the growing influence of socialist ideas within the nationalist movement.\nIt received support from sections of the Indian business community.\nTherefore, both statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"The 1930s witnessed growing debate over socialism and economic policy within the national movement.\nIndian business groups also became important participants in nationalist politics.\nEconomic ideology became increasingly significant in Congress politics during the 1930s."},
{"no":120,"text":"Among the following, who was not a proponent of the Bhakti cult?","A":"Nagarjuna","B":"Tukaram","C":"Tyagaraja","D":"Vallabhacharya","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Bhakti Movement","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Tukaram was a prominent Bhakti saint of Maharashtra.\nTyagaraja was an important devotional composer of South India.\nVallabhacharya founded the Pushtimarga tradition associated with Krishna devotion.\nNagarjuna was a major Buddhist philosopher associated with Madhyamaka, not the Bhakti tradition.","revisionPoints":"Nagarjuna: Buddhist philosopher; Madhyamaka school.\nTukaram: Varkari tradition, Maharashtra.\nTyagaraja: Carnatic music and Rama devotion.\nVallabhacharya: Pushtimarga/Vaishnavism.\nBhakti emphasized personal devotion to the chosen deity."},
{"no":121,"text":"For the Karachi Session of the Indian National Congress in 1931, presided over by Sardar Patel, who drafted the Resolution on Fundamental Rights and Economic Programme?","A":"Mahatma Gandhi","B":"Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru","C":"Dr. Rajendra Prasad","D":"Dr. B. R. Ambedkar","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Karachi Session 1931","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Karachi Session of the INC was held in 1931.\nThe session adopted an important resolution on Fundamental Rights and Economic Programme.\nAccording to the source, the resolution was drafted by Jawaharlal Nehru.","revisionPoints":"Karachi Session: 1931.\nPresident: Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.\nResolution dealt with:\nFundamental Rights.\nEconomic policy.\nCivil liberties.\nIt influenced the later constitutional framework."},
{"no":122,"text":"Who among the following were official Congress negotiators with the Cripps Mission?","A":"Mahatma Gandhi and Sardar Patel","B":"Acharya J. B. Kripalani and C. Rajagopalachari","C":"Pandit Nehru and Maulana Azad","D":"Dr. Rajendra Prasad and Rafi Ahmed Kidwai","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Cripps Mission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Cripps Mission arrived in India in 1942.\nIt attempted to obtain Indian cooperation during World War II.\nAccording to the source, Jawaharlal Nehru and Maulana Abul Kalam Azad were the official Congress negotiators.","revisionPoints":"Cripps Mission: 1942.\nHeaded by Sir Stafford Cripps.\nProposed constitutional arrangements after the war.\nCongress rejected the proposals."},
{"no":123,"text":"Which one of the following processes in living organisms is a digestive process?","A":"Breakdown of proteins into amino acids","B":"Breakdown of glucose into CO₂ and H₂O","C":"Conversion of glucose into glycogen","D":"Conversion of amino acids into proteins","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Digestion","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Digestion involves the breakdown of complex food molecules into simpler absorbable substances.\nProteins are digested into amino acids.\nGlucose breakdown is primarily associated with cellular respiration, while glycogen formation and protein synthesis are anabolic processes.","revisionPoints":"Proteins → amino acids.\nCarbohydrates → simple sugars.\nFats → fatty acids + glycerol.\nDigestion makes nutrients suitable for absorption."},
{"no":124,"text":"From the point of view of evolution of living organisms, which is the correct sequence?","A":"Otter → Tortoise → Shark","B":"Shark → Tortoise → Otter","C":"Tortoise → Shark → Otter","D":"Shark → Otter → Tortoise","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Evolution","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies Tortoise → Shark → Otter as the correct sequence.\nThe question tests evolutionary chronology based on the source's classification.\nTherefore, option (c) is the prescribed answer.","revisionPoints":"Evolution involves gradual diversification of life forms.\nComparative anatomy and fossils are important evidence for evolutionary studies.\nChronological questions should be answered according to the evolutionary sequence specified in the source."},
{"no":125,"text":"Consider the following:\nHepatitis B is several times more infectious than HIV/AIDS.\nHepatitis B can cause liver cancer.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Hepatitis B","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Hepatitis B virus (HBV) is highly infectious.\nChronic hepatitis B infection can cause cirrhosis and liver cancer.\nTherefore, both statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Hepatitis B is caused by HBV.\nIt affects the liver.\nChronic infection can lead to:\nCirrhosis.\nLiver cancer.\nHepatitis B can be prevented through vaccination."},
{"no":126,"text":"Excessive release of carbon monoxide (CO) may reduce oxygen supply in the human body. What causes this condition?","A":"CO is converted into CO₂ in the body.","B":"CO has a much higher affinity for haemoglobin than oxygen.","C":"CO destroys the chemical structure of haemoglobin.","D":"CO adversely affects the respiratory centre in the brain.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Carbon Monoxide","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Carbon monoxide binds strongly with haemoglobin.\nIt forms carboxyhaemoglobin.\nThis reduces the ability of blood to transport and release oxygen to tissues.\nTherefore, CO poisoning can cause severe tissue hypoxia.","revisionPoints":"CO is a colourless and odourless gas.\nProduced by incomplete combustion.\nCO + haemoglobin → carboxyhaemoglobin.\nCommon sources include vehicle exhaust and poorly ventilated combustion."},
{"no":127,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Swine Flu:\nEvery individual in the population is equally susceptible.\nAntibiotics have no role in the primary treatment of Swine Flu.\nTo prevent future spread in an epidemic area, all pigs must be culled.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Swine Flu","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Swine flu is caused by a virus, so antibiotics do not directly treat the viral infection.\nThe source rejects the claim that every person is equally susceptible.\nIt also does not support universal culling of pigs as the required prevention measure.","revisionPoints":"Swine flu is caused by influenza viruses.\nAntibiotics act against bacteria, not viruses.\nPrevention can include:\nVaccination where appropriate.\nHygiene.\nInfection-control measures.\nViral epidemics require public-health surveillance."},
{"no":128,"text":"With regard to transmission of HIV, which statement is not correct?","A":"Transmission from female to male is twice as likely as from male to female.","B":"Transmission risk is higher if a person has other sexually transmitted infections.","C":"An infected mother can transmit HIV during pregnancy, childbirth and breastfeeding.","D":"Transmission through infected blood is a significant route of infection.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["HIV Transmission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies statement (a) as incorrect.\nHIV transmission can occur through sexual contact, infected blood and mother-to-child transmission.\nOther sexually transmitted infections can increase the risk of HIV transmission.","revisionPoints":"HIV = Human Immunodeficiency Virus.\nMajor transmission routes:\nSexual transmission.\nInfected blood.\nMother-to-child transmission.\nHIV is not transmitted through casual contact."},
{"no":129,"text":"What are possible limitations of India in mitigating global warming at present and in the immediate future?\nAppropriate alternate technologies are not sufficiently available.\nIndia cannot invest huge funds in research and development.\nMany developed countries have already set up their polluting industries in India.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Global Warming Mitigation","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies limitations related to availability of suitable alternative technologies.\nIt also identifies constraints on making very large R&D investments.\nStatement 3 is not accepted as a direct limitation in the source.","revisionPoints":"Climate mitigation requires:\nClean technologies.\nRenewable energy.\nEnergy efficiency.\nResearch and innovation.\nDeveloping economies face technology and financing constraints.\nClimate policy involves balancing development and environmental objectives."},
{"no":130,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Commonwealth:\nThe Commonwealth has no charter, treaty or constitution.\nAll territories/countries once under the British Empire automatically became Commonwealth members.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Commonwealth","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source accepts statement 1.\nMembership of the Commonwealth is not automatic merely because a country was formerly part of the British Empire.\nTherefore, statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Commonwealth is a voluntary association of independent countries.\nMany members have historical links with the British Empire.\nMembership is not automatic.\nThe Commonwealth promotes cooperation in areas such as development and governance."},
{"no":131,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nAll colours are pleasant.\nSome colours are pleasant.\nNo colour is pleasant.\nSome colours are not pleasant.\nGiven that statement 4 is true, what can be definitely concluded?","A":"1 and 2 are true","B":"1 is false","C":"2 is false","D":"3 is true","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Syllogism","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Statement 4 says some colours are not pleasant.\nTherefore, it cannot be true that all colours are pleasant.\nHence statement 1 must be false.\nStatement 2 can still be true because some colours may be pleasant while others are not.","revisionPoints":"“All” is a universal statement.\n“Some” is an existential statement.\nIf even one exception exists, “all” cannot be true.\n“Some are not” does not imply “none are.”"},
{"no":132,"text":"In the context of bilateral trade negotiations between India and the European Union, consider:\nThe European Commission represents the EU in trade negotiations, whereas the European Council participates in legislation concerning economic policies.\nThe European Commission comprises Heads of State or Government, whereas the European Council comprises persons nominated by the European Parliament.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["European Commission","European Council","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies statement 1 as correct.\nThe European Commission is the EU's executive institution and plays a major role in external trade negotiations.\nStatement 2 incorrectly describes the composition of the two institutions.","revisionPoints":"European Commission: executive institution of the EU.\nEuropean Council: Heads of State or Government of member states.\nEuropean Parliament is directly elected.\nEU institutions have distinct roles in legislation, policy and external relations."},
{"no":133,"text":"What is the approximate representation of land-use classification in India?","A":"Net area sown 25%; forests 33%; other areas 42%","B":"Net area sown 58%; forests 17%; other areas 25%","C":"Net area sown 43%; forests 29%; other areas 28%","D":"Net area sown 47%; forests 23%; other areas 30%","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Land Use Classification","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source gives the approximate distribution as:\nNet area sown: 58%\nForests: 17%\nOther areas: 25%\nThese figures describe the broad land-use pattern presented in the source.","revisionPoints":"Land-use classification helps understand:\nAgricultural land.\nForest land.\nNon-agricultural uses.\nNet sown area refers to land sown at least once during an agricultural year.\nLand-use patterns vary by state due to climate, soil and economic factors."},
{"no":134,"text":"With reference to the National Investment Fund (NIF), consider:\nAssets in NIF are managed by the Union Ministry of Finance.\nNIF is maintained within the Consolidated Fund of India.\nCertain Asset Management Companies are appointed as fund managers.\nA certain proportion of annual income is used for financing selected social sectors.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"3 and 4","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["National Investment Fund","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 3 and 4 as correct.\nCertain Asset Management Companies were appointed to manage NIF assets.\nA portion of the income was intended to support selected social-sector schemes.\nStatements 1 and 2 are not accepted by the source.","revisionPoints":"NIF was associated with disinvestment proceeds.\nDisinvestment involves sale of government equity in public-sector enterprises.\nNIF was designed to channel returns toward specified purposes.\nSocial-sector investment was an important objective."},
{"no":135,"text":"In India, which of the following was regulated by the Forward Markets Commission?","A":"Currency futures trading","B":"Commodity futures trading","C":"Equity futures trading","D":"Both commodity futures and financial futures trading","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Forward Markets Commission","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The Forward Markets Commission (FMC) regulated commodity futures markets under the historical regulatory framework applicable to the question.\nIt dealt with organized futures trading in commodities.\nThe source identifies commodity futures trading as the answer.","revisionPoints":"FMC was associated with commodity futures regulation.\nCommodity futures involve contracts linked to commodities.\nThe FMC was later merged with SEBI in 2015.\nSEBI now regulates the relevant securities-market framework."},
{"no":136,"text":"Which one of the following is not a feature of a Limited Liability Partnership (LLP)?","A":"Partners should be less than 20","B":"Partnership and management need not be separate","C":"Internal governance may be decided by mutual agreement among partners","D":"It is a corporate body with perpetual succession","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["Limited Liability Partnership","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies the requirement that partners must be fewer than 20 as not a feature of LLPs.\nAn LLP is a separate legal entity with perpetual succession.\nIts internal governance can largely be determined through the LLP agreement.","revisionPoints":"LLP = Limited Liability Partnership.\nCombines features of:\nPartnership.\nCorporate legal personality.\nPartners generally have limited liability.\nLLP has perpetual succession."},
{"no":137,"text":"With reference to the institution of Banking Ombudsman in India, which statement is not correct?","A":"The Banking Ombudsman is appointed by the Reserve Bank of India.","B":"The Banking Ombudsman can consider complaints from NRIs having accounts in India.","C":"The orders passed by the Banking Ombudsman are final and binding on the parties concerned.","D":"The service provided by the Banking Ombudsman is free of any fee.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Banking Ombudsman","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies statement (c) as incorrect.\nThe Banking Ombudsman mechanism was established by the RBI to provide a relatively accessible grievance-redress mechanism for banking customers.\nThe source states that its service is free of charge.","revisionPoints":"Banking Ombudsman is an RBI-established grievance mechanism.\nIt deals with specified banking complaints.\nCustomers can approach the mechanism without paying a fee under the relevant framework.\nBanking grievance mechanisms are part of consumer protection in financial services."},
{"no":138,"text":"With reference to India, consider:\nNationalization of banks\nFormation of Regional Rural Banks\nAdoption of villages by bank branches\nWhich of the above can be considered steps taken to achieve financial inclusion?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Financial Inclusion","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Financial inclusion seeks to expand access to banking and financial services.\nBank nationalization expanded banking infrastructure.\nRegional Rural Banks were created to serve rural and agricultural communities.\nAdoption of villages by bank branches also helped extend banking services.","revisionPoints":"Financial inclusion: access to affordable financial services.\nImportant components:\nBank accounts.\nCredit.\nInsurance.\nPayments.\nRRBs focus particularly on rural areas.\nBranch expansion helps reach underserved populations."},
{"no":139,"text":"What was the immediate reason for Ahmad Shah Abdali to invade India and fight the Third Battle of Panipat?","A":"He wanted to avenge the expulsion by the Marathas of his viceroy Timur Shah from Lahore.","B":"Adina Beg Khan invited him to invade Punjab.","C":"He wanted to punish the Mughal administration for non-payment of revenues.","D":"He wanted to annex the fertile plains of Punjab up to the borders of Delhi.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Ahmad Shah Abdali","Third Battle of Panipat","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies territorial expansion towards the fertile plains of Punjab and Delhi as the immediate reason.\nAhmad Shah Abdali was the ruler of the Durrani Empire.\nHis conflict with the Marathas culminated in the Third Battle of Panipat in 1761.","revisionPoints":"Third Battle of Panipat: 1761.\nMain sides:\nMaratha Confederacy.\nAhmad Shah Abdali's forces.\nIt took place near Panipat, Haryana.\nThe battle had major consequences for the political balance of northern India."},
{"no":140,"text":"With reference to Pondicherry (Puducherry), consider:\nThe first European power to occupy Pondicherry was the Portuguese.\nThe second European power to occupy Pondicherry was the French.\nThe English never occupied Pondicherry.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Pondicherry","European Powers","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"According to the source, the Portuguese were the first European power to occupy Pondicherry.\nThe French later established an important colonial settlement there.\nThe English also occupied Pondicherry at certain periods, making statement 3 incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Puducherry became an important French colonial centre.\nFrench colonial expansion in India was associated with figures such as Dupleix.\nAnglo-French rivalry was prominent in 18th-century India.\nPuducherry is now a Union Territory of India."},
{"no":141,"text":"Why did Buddhism start declining in India in the early medieval times?\nBuddha was by that time considered as one of the incarnations of Vishnu and thus became part of Vaishnavism.\nThe invading tribes from Central Asia till the time of the last Gupta king adopted Hinduism and persecuted Buddhists.\nThe Kings of Gupta dynasty were strongly opposed to Buddhism.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Decline of Buddhism","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies statement 1 as the correct explanation.\nBuddha's incorporation into the Vaishnava tradition contributed to the religious assimilation of Buddhism.\nThe source does not accept statements 2 and 3.\nGupta rulers were not uniformly opposed to Buddhism.","revisionPoints":"Buddhism emerged in the 6th century BCE.\nBuddha was subsequently incorporated into some Hindu traditions as an avatar of Vishnu.\nGupta-period India witnessed interaction between Hindu and Buddhist traditions.\nBuddhism's decline had multiple social, economic, institutional and religious factors."},
{"no":142,"text":"Consider the following functions of commercial banks in India:\nPurchase and sale of shares and securities on behalf of customers.\nActing as executors and trustees of wills.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Commercial Banks Functions","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Commercial banks perform several agency services for their customers.\nThey may purchase and sell securities on behalf of customers.\nThey can also act as executors and trustees under appropriate arrangements.\nHence both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Major commercial bank functions:\nAccept deposits.\nGrant loans.\nCreate credit.\nProvide agency services.\nAgency services are performed on behalf of customers.\nBanks can facilitate investment and financial transactions."},
{"no":143,"text":"In India, the tax proceeds of which one of the following, as a percentage of gross tax revenue, had significantly declined in the last five years?","A":"Service tax","B":"Personal income tax","C":"Excise duty","D":"Corporation tax","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Gross Tax Revenue","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies excise duty as the tax whose share had significantly declined.\nExcise duty is an indirect tax.\nChanges in India's taxation structure affected the relative contribution of different taxes.","revisionPoints":"Excise duty: indirect tax.\nPersonal income tax: direct tax.\nCorporation tax: direct tax.\nTax composition changes with economic growth and tax reforms."},
{"no":144,"text":"Which authority makes recommendations to the Governor of a State regarding principles for determining taxes and duties that may be appropriated by Panchayats?","A":"District Planning Committees","B":"State Finance Commission","C":"Finance Ministry of that State","D":"Panchayati Raj Ministry of that State","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["State Finance Commission","Panchayats","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The State Finance Commission examines the financial position of Panchayats and Municipalities.\nIt recommends principles for distribution and assignment of financial resources.\nIts recommendations are made to the Governor of the State.","revisionPoints":"State Finance Commission → Article 243-I.\nConstituted by the Governor.\nDeals with finances of:\nPanchayats.\nMunicipalities.\nImportant for fiscal decentralization."},
{"no":145,"text":"Consider:\nIn India, taxes on transactions in Stock Exchanges and Futures Markets are:\nlevied by the Union\ncollected by the States\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Securities Transaction Tax","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as correct.\nThe question tests the constitutional distinction between levying and collecting taxes.\nUnder the framework applicable to the question, the Union levies the tax while collection is assigned to the States.","revisionPoints":"Tax powers are divided between Union and States.\nThe Seventh Schedule is central to this distribution.\nDistinguish:\nLevy.\nCollection.\nAppropriation.\nDistribution."},
{"no":146,"text":"In India, during the last decade, the total cultivated land for which crop remained more or less stagnant?","A":"Rice","B":"Oilseeds","C":"Pulses","D":"Sugarcane","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Cultivated Land","Pulses","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies pulses as the crop whose cultivated area remained relatively stagnant.\nPulses are important for food and nutritional security.\nIncreasing pulse production and productivity has been an important agricultural policy concern.","revisionPoints":"Major pulses include:\nGram.\nTur/arhar.\nMoong.\nUrad.\nLentil.\nPulses are important sources of plant protein.\nThey generally require less water than many cereal crops.\nIndia is a major producer and consumer of pulses."},
{"no":147,"text":"Consider:\nThe Union Government fixes the Statutory Minimum Price of sugarcane for each sugar season.\nSugar and sugarcane are essential commodities under the Essential Commodities Act.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Statutory Minimum Price","Sugarcane","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as correct.\nGovernment intervention in sugarcane pricing provides a framework for price protection to growers.\nSugar and sugarcane are covered by the relevant essential-commodity regulatory framework.","revisionPoints":"Sugarcane is an important cash crop.\nSugar industry has strong links with agriculture.\nGovernment regulates aspects of the sugar sector.\nSugarcane pricing affects both farmers and sugar mills."},
{"no":148,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider:\nGDP has increased by four times in the last 10 years.\nThe percentage share of the Public Sector in GDP has declined in the last 10 years.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["GDP Growth","Public Sector Share","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The source does not accept the claim that GDP increased fourfold in the specified period.\nIt accepts that the percentage share of the public sector in GDP declined.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"GDP: value of final goods and services produced within an economy.\nPublic-sector share and absolute public-sector output are different concepts.\nA sector's share can decline even while its absolute output increases.\nEconomic structure changes with liberalization and structural transformation."},
{"no":149,"text":"Consider the following that can be found in the ambient atmosphere:\nSoot\nSulphur hexafluoride\nWater vapour\nWhich of the above contribute to warming of the atmosphere?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Greenhouse Gases","Soot","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"Water vapour is an important natural greenhouse gas.\nSulphur hexafluoride (SF₆) is a very potent greenhouse gas.\nSoot/black carbon absorbs solar radiation and can contribute to atmospheric warming.\nTherefore, all three contribute to warming according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Major greenhouse gases include:\nWater vapour.\nCarbon dioxide.\nMethane.\nNitrous oxide.\nOzone.\nFluorinated gases.\nSF₆ has a very high global-warming potential.\nBlack carbon strongly absorbs solar radiation.\nGreenhouse warming and ozone depletion are different environmental issues."},
{"no":150,"text":"The International Development Association (IDA), a lending agency, is administered by:","A":"International Bank for Reconstruction and Development","B":"International Fund for Agricultural Development","C":"United Nations Development Programme","D":"United Nations Industrial Development Organization","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["International Development Association","UPSC","2010"],"expl":"The International Development Association (IDA) is part of the World Bank Group.\nThe source identifies the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) as the administering institution.\nIDA provides concessional financial assistance primarily to poorer countries.","revisionPoints":"IDA: International Development Association.\nIBRD: International Bank for Reconstruction and Development.\nBoth belong to the World Bank Group.\nIDA provides softer/concessional financing to low-income countries.\nIBRD generally serves middle-income and creditworthy countries.\nTop of Form\nBottom of Form"},
];
